WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.909
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:16.203
Unit two: Postcolonial Theory. Today you will analyse identity using passive nominal constructions.

4
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:19.341
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.614
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.869
Colonial legacy.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:31.495
Colonial legacy means the lasting social and political effects of colonial rule.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:35.859
The colonial legacy still shapes borders and languages across the region.

9
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.973
Your turn. Say: Colonial legacy.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.368
Colonial legacy.

11
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:46.048
Subaltern.

12
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.000
Subaltern means a group excluded from power whose voice is rarely heard.

13
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.963
Scholars ask whether the subaltern can truly speak.

14
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.133
Your turn. Say: Subaltern.

15
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:01.886
Subaltern.

16
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:03.744
Hybridity.

17
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.352
Hybridity means the mixing of cultures to form new identities.

18
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.867
Hybridity is visible in food, language and art.

19
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:15.170
Your turn. Say: Hybridity.

20
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:18.988
Hybridity.

21
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:20.589
Othering.

22
00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:25.023
Othering means treating a group as fundamentally different and lesser.

23
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:28.928
Othering turns difference into a justification for domination.

24
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:31.890
Your turn. Say: Othering.

25
00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:35.630
Othering.

26
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:37.802
Decolonization.

27
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:43.510
Decolonization means the process of undoing colonial control and its ideas.

28
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:48.543
Decolonization includes reclaiming history, not just territory.

29
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.348
Your turn. Say: Decolonization.

30
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.503
Decolonization.

31
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.588
Cultural hegemony.

32
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:04.555
Cultural hegemony means dominance achieved by making one culture's values seem natural.

33
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.438
Cultural hegemony makes dominance look like common sense.

34
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.376
Your turn. Say: Cultural hegemony.

35
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:16.768
Cultural hegemony.

36
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:18.657
Orientalism.

37
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:25.725
Orientalism means Western stereotypes that portray Eastern societies as exotic and inferior.

38
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:29.651
Edward Said exposed orientalism in Western literature.

39
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.100
Your turn. Say: Orientalism.

40
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.039
Orientalism.

41
00:02:37.783 --> 00:02:39.609
Diasporic identity.

42
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:45.321
Diasporic identity means a sense of self formed by living away from one's homeland.

43
00:02:45.917 --> 00:02:49.042
Diasporic identity blends loyalty to two places.

44
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.802
Your turn. Say: Diasporic identity.

45
00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.724
Diasporic identity.

46
00:02:58.483 --> 00:02:59.542
Agency.

47
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:05.069
Agency means the capacity to act independently and make one's own choices.

48
00:03:05.683 --> 00:03:09.297
Postcolonial writers restore agency to those once silenced.

49
00:03:09.883 --> 00:03:12.536
Your turn. Say: Agency.

50
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.378
Agency.

51
00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:18.617
Narrative voice.

52
00:03:19.617 --> 00:03:23.091
Narrative voice means the perspective from which a story is told.

53
00:03:23.683 --> 00:03:27.726
A shift in narrative voice lets colonised people tell their own story.

54
00:03:28.317 --> 00:03:31.642
Your turn. Say: Narrative voice.

55
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.977
Narrative voice.

56
00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:40.789
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.598
Visal, why do scholars still discuss colonialism when the empires ended decades ago?

58
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:57.963
Because identity is often shaped by an unresolved colonial legacy. Languages, borders and even school curricula were designed by colonisers.

59
00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.259
But people have moved on, surely.

60
00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:12.886
Politically, perhaps. Culturally, the subaltern is still rarely heard, and entire societies are frequently described through the eyes of outsiders.

61
00:04:13.983 --> 00:04:15.391
So what is the remedy?

62
00:04:16.483 --> 00:04:27.028
It is argued that decolonization must be intellectual as well as political, and that local narratives should be given authority over imported ones.

63
00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.818
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:34.540
Grammar focus: Passive nominal constructions.

65
00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.190
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:36.883 --> 00:04:40.613
Identity is often shaped by an unresolved colonial legacy.

67
00:04:41.517 --> 00:04:44.875
Local voices have long been silenced by cultural hegemony.

68
00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:49.164
History must be rewritten from the perspective of the colonised.

69
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:59.036
The passive foregrounds what is affected and can omit the agent. Use 'by + agent' only when it matters: Borders were drawn (by whom?) without local consent.

70
00:04:59.783 --> 00:05:04.688
Be careful! Don't say: Identity shapes often by colonial legacy.

71
00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:10.456
Say: Identity is often shaped by colonial legacy.

72
00:05:10.756 --> 00:05:13.463
Passive = be + past participle.

73
00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:16.838
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:17.633 --> 00:05:19.331
Voices from the Margins.

75
00:05:19.631 --> 00:05:26.634
Postcolonial theory examines how colonial rule continues to shape culture, language and identity long after independence.

76
00:05:27.633 --> 00:05:35.043
Edward Said argued that Western writers had constructed an imagined 'Orient', which was portrayed as exotic, static and inferior.

77
00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:42.712
Such othering was justified by cultural hegemony, through which the coloniser's values were presented as universal and natural.

78
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:52.213
In response, writers have been recovering narrative voice for the subaltern, and hybridity has been celebrated as a creative force rather than a loss of authenticity.

79
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.275
Decolonization is therefore understood not as a single event but as a continuing struggle to restore agency.

80
00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:04.810
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.917
Identity blank by colonial history.

82
00:06:08.217 --> 00:06:12.195
A, shapes. B, is often shaped. C, has shape.

83
00:06:15.733 --> 00:06:17.826
The answer is B: is often shaped.

84
00:06:18.983 --> 00:06:22.074
A group excluded from power and rarely heard is blank.

85
00:06:22.374 --> 00:06:26.306
A, hybrid. B, diasporic. C, subaltern.

86
00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:32.064
The answer is C: subaltern.

87
00:06:33.217 --> 00:06:36.389
Treating a group as fundamentally different and lesser is blank.

88
00:06:36.689 --> 00:06:40.528
A, othering. B, agency. C, hybridity.

89
00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:45.753
The answer is A: othering.

90
00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:48.568
Choose the correct passive.

91
00:06:48.868 --> 00:06:55.308
A, Borders were drawn without consent.. B, Borders drew without consent.. C, Borders were drawing without consent..

92
00:06:58.867 --> 00:07:02.027
The answer is A: Borders were drawn without consent..

93
00:07:03.183 --> 00:07:06.773
According to the reading, decolonization is understood as…

94
00:07:07.073 --> 00:07:12.271
A, a single event. B, a continuing struggle. C, a finished process.

95
00:07:15.833 --> 00:07:18.831
The answer is B: a continuing struggle.

96
00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:23.289
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.589
How does colonial history continue to shape identity today?

98
00:07:44.383 --> 00:07:45.710
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:46.010 --> 00:08:23.326
Colonial history shapes identity today through language, education and the stories societies tell about themselves. Many curricula were designed by outsiders, so young people sometimes learn their history as spectators rather than participants. Borders drawn without local consent still cause conflict. At the same time, hybridity has produced remarkable creativity in food, music and literature. I believe the healthiest response is neither denial nor resentment, but a confident reclaiming of narrative voice.

100
00:08:23.626 --> 00:08:25.323
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:26.217 --> 00:08:30.796
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive nominal constructions.

102
00:08:31.096 --> 00:08:36.326
Today's value is historical awareness. Know the past so you can understand the present.

103
00:08:36.626 --> 00:08:39.232
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

104
00:08:39.532 --> 00:08:42.207
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

